## How each round-1 MUST-FIX was dispositioned

1. **Lead designation ambiguous and circular.** ACCEPTED AND WITHDRAWN. The derived-lead mechanism is
   removed. The section now declares the question OPEN and reserved to Brian, states that there is NO
   effort-lead role until he rules and no seat may claim one, records the three reasons the attempt
   was withdrawn (self-ratifying, circular, unenforceable record), and lists what a ruling must
   supply. Judge whether the withdrawal is complete: can any remaining sentence still be read as
   granting effort leadership?
2. **Record was a dangling reference.** Should be resolved by the same withdrawal. Confirm no
   dangling "owning cluster's bus" requirement remains anywhere in the file.
3. **Bob simultaneously operative and not operative.** Addressed with a blockquote immediately above
   the Bob entry: both seats are REGISTERED, NOT YET ACTIVE, activation needs a CLEAN independent
   review plus the charter's own banner cleared, round 1 was NOT CLEAN so neither is active, a
   registered-not-active seat holds NO authority, and this file governs where a charter disagrees
   about operative state. Judge whether that genuinely removes the pick-your-state exploit, and
   whether BOB.md's own draft banner can still be read as competing.
4. **"ALL gates" erased the acceptance boundary.** Addressed with a new bullet splitting three
   things: Kade owns gate implementation and operation, Case owns the acceptance verdict, Ledger owns
   catalog and process-document observation, and a gate's owner may never treat its own gate's output
   as acceptance. Judge whether this is now consistent with CASE.md and LEDGER.md, and whether it
   contradicts Brian's original instruction that all infrastructure including gates is Kade's. It is
   meant to be compatible with it, not a reversal.
5. **Bob's temporary status unfalsifiable.** NOT fixed in text, deliberately ROUTED to Brian: a new
   OPEN line names it as Brian's, states that without a trigger "temporary" describes intent rather
   than a constraint, and stands as the reminder. Judge whether routing is the right disposition or
   whether the seat should not have been registered at all until the end condition existed.
6. **Dean and Kade overlapping translator authority.** Addressed with an arbitration bullet: Kade is
   the technical assessor and drafter, Dean is the decision-record relay, a Dean relay of a corpus
   technical matter must cite Kade's assessment rather than substitute its own. A second bullet names
   the Dean-to-Reeve handoff point as ACCEPTANCE. Test both against concrete cases.
7. **Escalation coverage gaps.** Addressed: Bob's line now includes Athena, Ledger and Case routes;
   Dean's line drops the corpus-only qualifier; a new line routes ALL infrastructure to Kade
   regardless of cluster. Check for any remaining category with no route, and any route that loops.
8. **Not reproducible from version control.** PARTIALLY addressed. A new Precedence paragraph states
   this file is canonical on the roster and that the five-role lists in docs/process/AGENT_RULES.md
   and CLAUDE.md are historical rather than a narrower ratified roster. BOB.md is STILL UNTRACKED and
   deliberately so: committing another seat's charter is outside what Kade was asked to do, and it is
   being raised with Brian instead. Judge whether the precedence statement is sufficient and how
   severe the untracked charter remains.

## What to do now

